1. Rust crates compiled into the `rift` binary

The binary contains the Rust crates listed with their full license texts in
THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md (in the Docker image: /usr/share/doc/rift/). They are
licensed under the MIT, Apache-2.0, ISC, BSD-3-Clause, Unicode-3.0 and Zlib
licenses, all of which permit use in an MIT-licensed program.

The crate libsqlite3-sys embeds the SQLite library, which its authors have
dedicated to the public domain (https://sqlite.org/copyright.html).


2. Debian packages in the Docker image

The Docker image (weneten/rift) is built on the official debian:bookworm-slim
image and adds the unmodified Debian packages ca-certificates, iproute2,
iptables, nftables and wireguard-tools together with their dependencies.
The `rift` binary is dynamically linked against the GNU C Library (glibc,
LGPL-2.1-or-later) from that base image.

These packages keep their own licenses. Several of them, among them iproute2,
iptables, nftables and wireguard-tools, are licensed under the GNU General
Public License version 2. RIFT runs these tools as separate programs and does
not link against them.

- License texts: /usr/share/doc/<package>/copyright inside the image.
- Exact package and source versions: /usr/share/doc/rift/debian-packages.txt
  inside the image (written when the image is built).
- Corresponding source code: every listed source version is available from
  the Debian archive, for example with `apt-get source <package>=<version>`
  or from https://snapshot.debian.org/.
